A||||2||DrDave||too much current draw, Help!||||9||
Z||000000||DrDave||07-02-2001||12:15 AM||||When I hook up the battery, I instantly start drawing current.  with a freshly charged battery, it's about 400 milliamps.<BR>   Unhooked the alternator totally.  removed all fuses and ckt breakers. Un plugged most of the connectors under the console and dash. Also removed the plug from the front compartment.  Disconnected the computor. removed all the light bulbs.  Next is to unhook the radio and heater and any connectors behind them.  After that I'm out of ideas?????????||||reg||9
Z||000001||terryk||07-02-2001||12:31 AM||||Is the fuel pump running? A bad oil pressure sender/switch will do that. <P>Remove each fuse in a turn until you find the circuit that is drawing the current.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||07-02-2001||09:58 AM||||Doc,  From your description of what you have done, the only juice bleeding from the batt would come from the fusible link or the starter.  Other than that you've disconnected everything else.  I have an '87 manual and I assume you car should be the same except maybe an '84 but I don't have that manual so I'm not positive.  Anyway, on the V6 the fusible link feeds: headlight door modules; ECM  pin B1 on connector 2 and pin C16 on connector 1; the alt (you've already unhooked); cold start circuit (switch & injector); direct to solenoid (not fused by wire or fuse).<BR>L4 - is the same except locations on ECM and no cold start circuit: ECM pins 10 & 15 on connector 1.<BR>Hope this helps. GL||||reg||1
